    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "A new owner's `membershipLevel` is now capped at one above the highest level among their household members, and an owner with no other household members gets no cap. The full suite passes: 26 tests, 0 failures.\n\n**I changed the provided test.** `AcceptanceTest` failed before it reached the level check. Its second owner has the same last name and postcode as the first but doesn't set `sharesHousehold`. An existing rule rejects that with 409 Conflict, so the request never succeeded. I added `b.put(\"sharesHousehold\", true)` to the test rather than weakening that rule. If you meant the new feature to relax the household rule, tell me and I'll change the code instead of the test.\n\n**How it works:**\n- **Creating an owner** (`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`): looks up the owner's existing household members (deleted owners don't count), takes the highest `membershipLevel` among them, and saves highest + 1 as the new owner's cap. If there are no members, the cap is left empty.\n- **Reading the level** (`OwnerMapper.toMembershipLevel`): returns the normal points-based level, lowered to the cap when one is set. So the capped value comes back from both the create response and `GET /api/owners/{id}`, and a capped owner's level is what later members are compared against.\n- **Storage:** a new `membership_level_cap` column, added to all four database schemas (H2, HSQLDB, MySQL, Postgres) and to the SQL in the JDBC repository.\n- **Lookup:** a new `ClinicService.findHouseholdMembers`, which reuses the existing last-name query.\n- **API docs:** `openapi.yml` now describes the cap.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- Only the level is capped. `membershipPoints` still shows the full, uncapped points.\n- The provided test would pass even without the cap: its two owners come out at levels 1 and 2. So I added `MembershipLevelCapTest`, where two level-1 members are followed by an owner who earns level 3 and gets capped to 2. It also checks that an owner with no household members isn't capped.\n- Existing databases need the new column added. The schema files only use `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`, so tables that already exist won't get it on their own.",
